From 49bcdd692d1dc5e1cf178ee59e1fd6cd8cc49f7a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 14:54:38 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] End box layout, silence the game at the menu, drop the Save
 button

Win panel:
- Portraits 8px right. The colour block is drawn at 8 * column while everything
  else in a popup is drawn at 8 * column + 8, so a portrait on what looks like
  the middle column actually sat 8px left of centre. Each starting column moves
  one right: one face is now 48..96, centred on 72; two are 16..64 and 80..128,
  the pair centred on 72; three 40-wide blocks tile 8..128, centred on 68, the
  closest an 8px grid gets.
- Tick to the bottom left corner, column 0, with its clickmap row moved to match
  (x 0..16, which is exactly the icon's own extent in clickmap space).

Menu no longer leaves the game running behind it. The start screen sits OVER the
game rather than replacing it, and viewport.set_enabled(false) only stops it
taking input - it carries on drawing, and drawing a burning building is what
plays Sfx.burning. After a victory the map is full of them, so the fire crackled
away behind the menu forever. Taking Menu now stands the cheat's soldiers down,
freezes the game so the burn counters stop advancing (which stops the retrigger
at source) and stops every sound effect. The music is left alone. Freezing costs
nothing here because the game is over; taking the tick instead leaves it running,
which is the point of that choice.

audio_stop_sfx() is a stop, not a fade. A real fade needs the handle of each
playing instance and this audio layer keeps none - play_sfx and cf_play both
throw the return of audio_play_sound away. Fading by asset instead would leave
the gain at zero for every later play of that sound, which is a worse bug than
an abrupt stop.

The Save button is gone - enum entry, clickmap row, label and field. Progress was
always written the moment a mission was won, so the button only ever offered a
re-save of something already on disk.

Why a completed mission may not have shown: only a game that carries a mission
index can be ticked off, and that index is set when GameInitBox starts a mission.
A game LOADED from a save written before the field existed comes back as the
constructor's -1 and records nothing. That case now says so in the log instead of
failing silently. The progress array is also written straight into the global
rather than through a local holding the same array - the indirection was doing
nothing and left the whole thing resting on array reference semantics.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
